<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Urinary retention (bladder scan 640 mL after voiding) with severe lower urinary tract symptoms and a raised creatinine in a 79-year-old man, now drained by an indwelling catheter and started on tamsulosin. Please confirm the cause, judge the risk, plan a trial without catheter, advise on further medical treatment, and set out the procedures that would suit him, given that he is frail and wants to avoid an operation if he can. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Soon, within two weeks; bladder drained today. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Is benign prostatic obstruction the cause of his retention, and is it high-risk or low-risk chronic retention?</item><item>When should we try him without the catheter, and on what medicines?</item><item>Should he start a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor, and how should we read his PSA?</item><item>If the trial fails, which procedures suit a frail 79-year-old who fears an operation, including less invasive ones and those under local anesthesia?</item><item>Should he have an indwelling catheter, intermittent catheterization, or a suprapubic catheter in the meantime?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Tagore is seen at the request of Dr. Sengupta two weeks after an indwelling catheter was placed for retention and tamsulosin 0.4 mg at bedtime was started. He has slept through since, has had no fever, pain, or blood in the urine, and no dizziness or falls. Before the catheter he had months of obstructive and storage symptoms and a febrile urinary infection five months ago. He has no history of urethral stricture, pelvic surgery or radiation, neurological disease, or diabetes, and takes no anticholinergic or decongestant medicines. He is courteous and precise, and he says plainly that he is more afraid of an operation than of his illness; he wants to understand each choice. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Rabindranath Tagore was born on 7 May 1861 in the Jorasanko mansion in Calcutta, the youngest son of Debendranath Tagore, a leader of the Brahmo Samaj; the name Tagore is the anglicised transliteration of Thakur. [D]</item><item>He was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1913 &quot;because of his profoundly sensitive, fresh and beautiful verse, by which, with consummate skill, he has made his poetic thought, expressed in his own English words, a part of the literature of the West&quot;. [D]</item><item>He started a school at Santiniketan that began formally on 22 December 1901 with five students, and around it he built Visva-Bharati, an unconventional university. [D]</item><item>He was known by the sobriquets Gurudeb, Kobiguru, and Bishwokobi. [D]</item><item>His last five years were marked by chronic pain and two long periods of illness: in late 1937 he lost consciousness and remained comatose and near death for a time, and in late 1940 a similar spell followed, from which he never recovered. [D]</item><item>On 15 September 1940, while visiting Kalimpong, he fell seriously ill with severe pain in his lower abdomen; doctors diagnosed a severe urinary tract infection, and he was brought back, unconscious, to Calcutta. [D]</item><item>In the last two years of his life illness confined him to the house, with swollen feet, numbness of the fingers, and frequent fevers; women of his family and household nursed him at home. [D]</item><item>He sought cure in allopathy, homeopathy, and ayurvedic medicine; physicians including Bidhan Chandra Roy and Nilratan Sarkar supervised his treatment; he is reported to have refused surgery despite repeated insistence from his doctors, to have sought a herbal cure, and to have said that he was more afraid of complicated medical treatment than of death. [D]</item><item>He suffered the untimely deaths of his wife and three of his children; he took his daughter Renuka to hill stations in the Himalayas for her recovery from tuberculosis. [D]</item></list><paragraph>7 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

