<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Urinary retention, cause now established (R33.9). Bladder outlet obstruction from benign prostatic hyperplasia, without a neurological cause or stricture. [S]</item><item>Benign prostatic hyperplasia with chronic urinary retention (N40.1). An 85 mL gland with a median lobe, benign by examination. PSA density about 0.06 fits benign enlargement; with his age, his goals, and a benign examination, no prostate MRI or biopsy is advised. The retention is chronic and was symptomatic. The raised creatinine has recovered with drainage and there is no hydronephrosis, so it is low risk now that it is drained. The trabeculated bladder suggests long-standing obstruction, and a first trial without catheter may fail. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: IPSS 26 and quality-of-life score 5; bladder scan 640 mL; creatinine 1.42 mg/dL at presentation and 1.18 mg/dL today (eGFR 63); PSA 4.9 ng/mL drawn before catheterization; urinalysis and the negative intake urine culture; today&apos;s ultrasound: no hydronephrosis, trabeculated bladder, prostate about 85 mL with a modest median lobe. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Start finasteride 5 mg daily now: with an 85 mL prostate it should shrink the gland by about a quarter over three to six months; double later PSA values for interpretation, with a new baseline at about six months. [S]</item><item>Continue tamsulosin 0.4 mg at bedtime; trial without catheter at about six weeks, once any infection is treated. [S]</item><item>Keep the indwelling urethral catheter, changed every four weeks, with closed drainage and no routine antibiotics; intermittent self-catheterization is the better option if he is willing and able, and a suprapubic catheter if long-term drainage is needed. [S]</item><item>If the trial fails, I would recommend an operation for refractory retention: laser enucleation (HoLEP) is the best choice for this size, under spinal or general anesthesia; transurethral resection or simple prostatectomy are alternatives. Prostatic artery embolization under local anesthesia is a less invasive option with lower and less durable success. Prostatic urethral lift and water vapor therapy are not well suited to this size, a median lobe, and retention. [S]</item><item>Before any decision, a geriatric assessment of frailty, gait, cognition, and delirium risk, and a frank discussion of his goals. [S]</item><item>If he declines a procedure after a failed trial, a long-term catheter or a second trial after three to six months of finasteride are reasonable choices; reconsider a procedure for recurrent infection, bleeding, stones, or kidney harm. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Sengupta the same day; Mr. Tagore agrees with starting finasteride and the plan for a trial without catheter, and wants time to think about any procedure. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Primary care manages the trial without catheter and the catheter care. Urology will discuss the next step after the trial, and will see him again before any procedure. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
